{
  "rules": [
    "Systematic Underestimation of Bullish Spikes: The model consistently fails to capture the magnitude of upward price breaks (e.g., the jump from ~82 to ~93), forecasting a flat or slightly rising trend while true prices surge. Apply a positive bias adjustment of +10 to +15 EUR when the 3-day price momentum exceeds 5%.",
    "Lag in Trend Reversal: Forecasts exhibit significant lag during the transition from consolidation to breakout, often predicting mean reversion to the 84-85 range while the market establishes a new higher floor. Delay the mean-reversion signal by at least 5-7 days after a confirmed breakout above 88 EUR.",
    "Sentiment Ignorance: Positive sentiment spikes (0.20-0.33) in the history are not reflected in the forecast slope. When sentiment history contains values > 0.20, increase the forecast slope by 0.5 EUR/day for the first 10 days of the horizon.",
    "Overshoot Correction on Volatility: The model assumes low volatility (smooth curves) while true outcomes show high volatility (±3-5 EUR swings). Introduce a volatility band of ±4 EUR around the forecast to account for the observed oscillation between 90 and 100 EUR.",
    "Horizon-Specific Bias: The error is minimal in the first 3 days but grows exponentially thereafter. Apply a non-linear correction factor that increases the forecast by 0.8% per day for days 4 through 15 to align with the accelerating trend."
  ]
}
